Hi Ken, yes, that is correct. Unfortunately, the majority of my sites were sampled only once, and only a small subset were sampled twice. Each site was sampled at 20 different locations, however. I've considered using a space-for-time substitution, using each of these locations within the site as replicates instead of repeat surveys, but my data frame would be quite large then. I should mention that the only way that I've been able to get my code to work as-is is by running penalized likelihood on the null models using optimizePenalty. Once I had outputs for my null models, I pooled the results with Rubin's rules, using AIC to account for differences in penalty assignments (see code below). Is this an appropriate approach, or should I try running the models with the space-for-time substitution instead?

#2. Fit the model with penalized likelihood (if needed)
#a. Define a function with various penalty values
set.seed(500)
fit_null_models_pen <- function(null_models){
  model_list <- list()
  for(i in seq_along(null_models)){
    model_list[[i]] <- unmarked::optimizePenalty(
      null_models[[i]],
      penalties = c(0.02, 0.1, 0.2, 0.33,1,2)
    )
  }
  return(model_list)
}

#b. Apply the function to the list of null models
null_models_pen <- fit_null_models_pen(null_models)
null_models_pen

weights <- (1/aic_values)
weights <- weights/sum(weights)

pooled_coefs <- rowSums(coefficients*weights)
pooled_se <- sqrt(rowSums(weights*(se^2 + (coefficients - pooled_coefs)^2)))
pooled_z <- pooled_coefs/pooled_se
pooled_p <- 2*(1-pnorm(abs(pooled_z)))

pool_results <- data.frame(
  Estimate = pooled_coefs,
  SE = pooled_se,
  Z = pooled_z,
  p_value = pooled_p
)
pool_results <- round(pool_results, digits = 5)

#c. Pool results with Rubin's rules for variance estimation
#1) Extract components of model outputs
coefficients <- sapply(null_models_pen, coef)
se <- sapply(null_models_pen, function(model) sqrt(diag(vcov(model))))
z_values <- coefficients/se
p_values <- 2*(1-pnorm(abs(z_values)))
aic_values <- sapply(null_models_pen, function(model) model@AIC)

#2) Calculate weights based on AIC values
weights <- (1/aic_values)
weights <- weights/sum(weights)

#3) Pool results
pooled_coefs <- rowSums(coefficients*weights)
pooled_se <- sqrt(rowSums(weights*(se^2 + (coefficients - pooled_coefs)^2)))
pooled_z <- pooled_coefs/pooled_se
pooled_p <- 2*(1-pnorm(abs(pooled_z)))

#4) Merge pooled results into one data frame
pool_results <- data.frame(
  Estimate = pooled_coefs,
  SE = pooled_se,
  Z = pooled_z,
  p_value = pooled_p
)
pool_results <- round(pool_results, digits = 5)
pool_results
