> > Thanks for your response. Of my 76 sites, only 5 were sampled twice and the rest were sampled once. However, within each site, there were multiple bait stations deployed (between 5 and 10) ~10m apart along 1-2 transects (i.e., total of up to 20 bait stations per site). The distance at which these were deployed should be sufficient as our study species are rodents with limited dispersal. Therefore, I end up with 76 sites with up to 20 repeat surveys using the space-for-time substitution. I presume based on your reply that this would be the desired approach compared to the one I have taken thus far?
> >
> > Best,
> > Kristin
> >
> > On Tuesday, October 22, 2024 at 4:09:01 AM UTC-7 Ken Kellner wrote:
> > > The answer depends somewhat on the sample sizes you're dealing with, i.e. how many sites with multiple replicate samples vs sites with only one sample. It's not obvious from your code what these numbers are. Repeated samples are a crucial part of this occupancy model. If you only have a handful of sites with multiple repeated samples I would not be confident in the estimates of detection probability and therefore would not be confident in the occupancy estimates either. This is particularly true for the multispecies version of the model with its added complexity. Penalized likelihood is not a solution to experimental design limitations (just because it removes NaNs doesn't mean I'd trust the numbers you get).
> > >
> > > Space for time to get the replicates might be a solution but it depends again on the design. Can the spatial replicates really be considered replicate samples of some underlying overarching "site"? It depends on your species, study design, knowledge of the system, etc. Also, what kind of sample size do you end up with after doing that? If you do a space for time substitution and end up with only a handful of 'sites' as a result, that's introducing a new problem. In general the multispecies model needs a large sample size (ideally ~hundreds of sites) to have reasonable statistical power.
> > >
> > > Ken
