> > > > I wanted to follow up with this. I have adjusted my code slightly to include starting values, increase maxit control, and use the Nelder-Mead method to avoid calculating the Hessian matrix. I no longer receive NaNs but I still have separation in my models, which I accounted for with penalized likelihood (essentially the same process as before but with a few extra steps). Unfortunately, I have fewer significant results with this code, but I believe it to be more robust than what I had initially developed. However, I am still not certain if this approach is valid or if I need to use a space-for-time substitution to account for the missing repeat surveys. Any recommendations?
> > > >
> > > > Updated code:
> > > > #1. Define a function to fit the model starting_values <- rnorm(14, mean = 0, sd = 0.2571) fit_null_models <- function(umf_list, stateformulas, detformulas){ set.seed(500) model_list <- list() for(i in seq_along(umf_list)){ model_list[[i]] <- occuMulti( stateformulas = stateformulas, detformulas = detformulas, control = list(maxit=20000), #Set number of iterations method = "Nelder-Mead", #Avoid Hessian is singular error starts = starting_values, data = umf_list[[i]] ) } return(model_list) } #2. Apply the function null_models <- fit_null_models(umf_list, stateformulas, detformulas) summary(null_models[[1]]) #NOTE: Model is a poor fit (possible separation), try using penalized likelihood to control model  #complexity and reduce variance of parameter estimates (Murphy 2012, Clipp et al. 2021) #3. Fit the model with penalized likelihood (if needed)  #a. Define a function with various penalty values fit_null_models_pen <- function(null_models){ set.seed(500) model_list <- list() for(i in seq_along(null_models)){ model_list[[i]] <- unmarked::optimizePenalty( null_models[[i]], penalties = c(0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.33, 1, 2) ) } return(model_list) } #b. Apply the function to the list of null models - code takes a while to run null_models_pen <- fit_null_models_pen(null_models) null_models_pen #4. Pool results with Rubin's rules for variance estimation #a. Extract components of model outputs coefficients <- sapply(null_models_pen, coef) se <- sapply(null_models_pen, function(model) sqrt(diag(vcov(model)))) z_values <- coefficients/se p_values <- 2*(1-pnorm(abs(z_values))) aic_values <- sapply(null_models_pen, function(model) model@AIC) #b. Calculate model weights based on AIC values weights <- (1/aic_values) weights <- weights/sum(weights) #c. Pool results pooled_coefs <- rowSums(coefficients*weights) pooled_se <- sqrt(rowSums(weights*(se^2 + (coefficients - pooled_coefs)^2))) pooled_z <- pooled_coefs/pooled_se pooled_p <- 2*(1-pnorm(abs(pooled_z))) #d. Merge pooled results into one data frame pool_results <- data.frame( Estimate = pooled_coefs, SE = pooled_se, Z = pooled_z, p_value = pooled_p ) pool_results <- round(pool_results, digits = 5) pool_results
